WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Speaker 1: So Republican Senator Rick&nbsp;
Scott wants America to get back to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.840
Now, it's a strange talking point to use when&nbsp;
unemployment is as low as it's essentially been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.600
for 50 years. So we're going to look at this&nbsp;
video. And the important thing to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.000
here is once you commit to certain talking points&nbsp;
as the Republican Party has, you can't change them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.720
based on what's going on. And we've seen this with&nbsp;
debt and deficit. We've got to balance the budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.440
Well, Republican presidents never do, so they&nbsp;
just stop talking about it. When a Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.240
is in office and blowing up the deficit. But they&nbsp;
don't abandon the talking point. They just don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.747
mention it anymore. Now that there's a Democrat&nbsp;
in the White House, they have to go to America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:42.400
has got to get back to work. We need people&nbsp;
working and people are working. And we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.400
going to look at those numbers. But check out Rick&nbsp;
Scott. When all you've got is a hammer, everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.120
looks like a nail or whatever you want to say.&nbsp;
But the talking point is Republicans get people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.920
to work. Democrats are lazy. And so when there's&nbsp;
a Democratic president, people don't work. Doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.360
matter if it doesn't line up with the facts.
Speaker 2: Here's what I believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.520
We've got to get Americans back to work. If you're&nbsp;
able bodied, you don't have young children to, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.000
know, incapacitated, dependent, get to work.
Speaker 1: Right. Just go and stop being lazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.440
If you're not handicapped, if you don't&nbsp;
have a disability, if you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.760
a physical issue. I get I mean, maybe they would&nbsp;
consider mental issues, too. I don't know. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.680
touch and go with Republicans. They claim to care&nbsp;
about mental health, but then they don't seem to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.880
You got to get to work because nobody's&nbsp;
working these days. And it's part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.600
culture and the economic policy of Biden.&nbsp;
And then you look at the unemployment rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.120
and you see that the unemployment rate right&nbsp;
now is essentially as low as it's been since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.840
the early 1970s. You have to go. I mean,&nbsp;
okay, you've had this brief period in 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:48.000
and then you've got to go all the way back to&nbsp;
the early 1970s to find a period during which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.920
unemployment was this low. Now, I know that&nbsp;
there are people going to hear me say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.320
and they'll say, But, David, the unemployment&nbsp;
rate tells us nothing. The truth is in the labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.520
participation rate, remember, we've talked about&nbsp;
this claim from the right for the last decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.160
and I've explained the labor participation rate&nbsp;
many times before and I will do it again. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.800
the labor participation rate is a more complicated&nbsp;
issue, and I'll tell you why in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.760
But when you look at the labor participation&nbsp;
rate, you'll find that it's right around 62%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:26.320
roughly where it was in 2015 as well. Now, why&nbsp;
isn't the labor participation rate a really great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.560
indicator for the health of the economy? Well,&nbsp;
there's a couple of different things. Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:39.200
the size of our population keeps growing. So&nbsp;
at almost any time period, you can accurately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.920
say there are more people than ever working and&nbsp;
there are more people than ever not working. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.480
what does that really tell us? But there's also&nbsp;
a few important things to consider. Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:58.000
we are in the midst of a significant retiring&nbsp;
phase for baby boomers. That takes people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.960
out of the labor force. Is is it an indicator the&nbsp;
economy is bad? No. In fact, you could argue it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:12.720
an indicator the economy is good when people can&nbsp;
afford to retire. It signals that you have an okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:19.200
economy. The other thing about this is that when&nbsp;
you look at the labor force participation rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.360
if you have full time parents who aren't&nbsp;
working by choice, that lowers the labor labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.320
participation rate. But that's a good thing when&nbsp;
the economy gets worse. Some of those full time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.840
parents say, hey, I can't afford not to work. I&nbsp;
have to go and get a job. Labor participation rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.960
goes up, but not because the economy's good. It's&nbsp;
actually because the economy is bad. And also, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:50.400
another reminder, if you're in college, undergrads&nbsp;
in college, if you're just in school full time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.120
you count against the labor participation&nbsp;
rate. But again, when there's a better economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:02.400
18 to 22 year olds can afford to go to school&nbsp;
full time instead of having to get a job to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.840
help their family, help their parents or whatever&nbsp;
the case may be. So labor participation rate not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:14.880
good indicator. Back to the main point we have&nbsp;
right now, significant levels of employment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.160
We have a labor participation rate that&nbsp;
is normal. It's not particularly high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:29.200
or particularly low. You have many companies&nbsp;
looking to hire and yes, we have high inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.520
which I believe has peaked and is going to start&nbsp;
coming down. Gas prices have already been coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.920
down for the last six weeks, etc. It is very hard&nbsp;
to argue that this is a disastrous economy. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:47.520
you can also talk about wages. Inflation adjusted&nbsp;
wages are actually doing okay. Have there been if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:54.000
you look at a 40 year period, stagnant wages,&nbsp;
has productivity growth dramatically outpaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.520
inflation adjusted wages? Yes. And that's a&nbsp;
bigger issue. That relates to technology and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.720
corporate power in so many different things.&nbsp;
But by the standards we use to look at wages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.600
We've seen some pretty okay wage increases. So&nbsp;
while they have not kept up with productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.040
there is no doubt that that indicator also isn't&nbsp;
the sign of this terrible economy. So Rick Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.000
and other Republicans. There has never been&nbsp;
a time. Even under Barack Obama during this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.360
massive recovery from the 2829 recession, even&nbsp;
then, when we started to see unemployment drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.880
the stock market explode, consumer confidence&nbsp;
go up, inflation very, very controlled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.600
Even then, they weren't willing to&nbsp;
accept it. And as I've said before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.400
many of these things only have a little bit to&nbsp;
do with the president anyway. But once you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.480
married to these talking points, you have to stick&nbsp;
with them. And that's exactly what we are seeing

